I didn't see this thread earlier, owing to all the cheap plastic imitations
of Usenet posts floating around. Sorry 'bout that.

Spanish NetHack uses ISO 8859-1 internally, but on Unix it has a user-
selectable option ("unicode") to convert to UTF-8 on output. This is a bit
of a pain to code properly, because the map symbols are still in IBM 437.
Text strings and map symbols follow mostly different code paths, but a map
symbol can appear outside the map in three places: in the response to the
'/' command, in the bottom line of the TTY and Win32 ports (if you change
the symbol for gold, the new symbol will be displayed next to the money you
have), and in the current setting of the "bouldersym" option.

I've experimented with going all-Unicode and plan to implement this when the
DevTeam releases the next version. (Doing it now would break binary
compatibility.) The big issues are: backspacing (you have to clear all
character bytes); mussing an engraving (careful or you'll create invalid
sequences); and such things as capitalization and recognizing when you have
a letter, a digit, and such.

